ant urban center that contributed to the cultural landscape of the region."}], "places": [{"name": "Kufa, Iraq", "role": "Cultural Center"}, {"name": "Baghdad, Iraq", "role": "Proximity to Kufa"}, {"name": "Najaf, Iraq", "role": "Nearby city"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Kufa", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "kufa"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q186234", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kufa"], "tags": ["Iraq", "big city", "Asia", "Architecture & Built Works", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Kufa left a notable mark on Classical history, documented across 76 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Kufa played a critical role in shaping early Islamic governance and jurisprudence, directly influencing the development of Islamic law through scholars like Abu Hanifa and serving as a battleground during the First Fitna, which shaped sectarian divides.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-05T05:53:29Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28731167326", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "City in iraq, about 170 kilometres (110 mi) south of baghdad, and 10 kilometres (6.2 mi) northeast of najaf. Kufa is a big city from Asia. Holds notable historical significance, documented across 76 language editions of Wikipedia. Relevant to cause-and-effect analysis.", "newValue": "Kufa, an ancient city located in present-day Iraq, serves as a critical nexus of Islamic history and culture. Established in 638 CE by the caliph Umar ibn al-Khattab, Kufa quickly became a pivotal center for Islamic scholarship, governance, and military strategy during the early Islamic period. Its geographic position, approximately 170 kilometers south of Baghdad, facilitated trade and communication across the region, connecting various cultures and facilitating the spread of Islamic teachings.

The city played a significant role during the First Fitna (656–661 CE), the first civil war within the Islamic community, where Kufa emerged as a stronghold for Ali ibn Abi Talib, the fourth caliph and cousin of Prophet Muhammad. Kufa's population actively participated in political and military decisions of the time, including the Battle of Siffin in 657 CE, which was pivotal in the power struggles that defined early Islamic governance. The establishment of Kufa as a center for Islamic jurisprudence led to the development of the Kufa school of thought, which significantly influenced Islamic law and theology.

The legacy of Kufa is profound, as it laid the groundwork for major Islamic intellectual traditions and movements. Scholars such as Abu Hanifa, the founder of the Hanafi school of Sunni jurisprudence, were integral to Kufa's educational landscape, which further propagated its influence throughout the Islamic world. Kufa's eventual decline in prominence did not erase its historical significance; rather, it evolved into a symbol of early Islamic unity and diversity, with its cultural and scholarly contributions reverberating through centuries."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-05T05:53:29Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28731167326", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": ["Cultural, artistic, or intellectual creation"], "newValue": ["The establishment of Kufa in 638 CE was part of the early Islamic expansion under Caliph Umar ibn al-Khattab.", "The strategic location of Kufa along trade routes facilitated its growth as a cultural and intellectual hub.", "The political turmoil following the death of Prophet Muhammad created a demand for new centers of governance and scholarship, which Kufa fulfilled."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-05T05:53:29Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28731167326", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": ["Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission"], "newValue": ["Kufa became a center of Islamic scholarship, influencing legal and theological thought through its schools and scholars.", "The city's involvement in the First Fitna highlighted the political divisions within early Islam, shaping future sectarian identities.", "Kufa's legacy continues to impact Islamic culture and education, as it is still referenced in discussions of Islamic jurisprudence and governance."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-05T05:53:29Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28731167326", "field": "detailsJson.relationships", "oldValue": [{"sourceSlug": "kufa", "sourceName": "Kufa", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "era-classical", "targetName": "Clas